Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,520
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,760
My guest today is Kenneth Schlenker, co-founder and CEO of Opel, the focus company.

8
00:00:50,640 --> 00:01:07,360
On the podcast, I talk with Kenneth about why retention is the only real mote, how dropping paid conversion from 20% to 9% increased revenue, and why he sees the rise in competition as a net positive for Opel.

9
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,640
Hey Kenneth, it's so great to have you back on the podcast.

10
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:12,640
Thanks so much for having me back.

11
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,600
So we've got a lot to catch up on.

12
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,280
You know, last time we spoke, you were at five million in ARR.

13
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:24,000
You since shared that you hit ten million in ARR pretty soon after that.

14
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,680
So you were scaling rapidly.

15
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,360
I now hit over a million daily active users.

16
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,240
So, I mean, the app just been taken off.

17
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:39,040
So I wanted to talk through some of that with you and, and, and, you know, see what it's taken to get there.

18
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:45,440
So, yeah, tell me first about, like, what it took from last we spoke at five million to get to that ten million.

19
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,880
Yeah, it sounds now a long time ago, but yeah, we had a great conversation last time.

20
00:01:51,280 --> 00:02:01,520
We're really in the process of scaling with, at the time, a hard pay wall, and we got to scale pretty efficiently with a small team.

21
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,440
I think we reached 10 million dollars annual created revenue of like 11 people back then.

22
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:10,960
But, you know, after that, what happened was we weren't growing as fast as we'd like in terms of users.

23
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:20,080
And, you know, for us, what really matters is to be able to create a product that will be used by a lot of people because we're addressing this massive issue.

24
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:28,480
And so, you know, we stopped kind of focusing on revenue, really focusing on what product we can build that can be a billion user product.

25
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:34,960
And so that's the trajectory that changed and happy to talk about the stuff that we've done to get there.

26
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:35,520
Yeah.

27
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:44,880
You know, one of the things, you just did a great talk at Tokyo App Growth Annual last week and you and I got to hang out a little bit in Tokyo.

28
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:52,800
And one of the things you shared in that talk was that you had a mindset shift from efficiency to company building.

29
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,160
Tell me a little bit about that.

30
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:54,560
Yeah.

31
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:02,720
Yeah, I think this is maybe a counterintuitive take, but you read a lot today about the billion dollar one person company.

32
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:14,320
If you buy into that, basically someone, AI can just, you know, VAD code their way to getting to, you know, ten million, twenty million, thirty million dollars in revenue and then create this massive company.

33
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:16,320
I think that's a lie.

34
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:26,240
I think that actually the companies that actually do reach a billion people and billions of valuations, they need more than one person.

35
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:27,440
They actually need a team.

36
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:29,360
You need a brand.

37
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:38,640
You need retention and AI can be used to kind of improve the value that the product gives to users.

38
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:43,200
And then, and only then, I think you can really kind of create massive, massive amounts of value.

39
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,720
And so, yeah, I think that was the topic of the talk, which I gave at the conference.

40
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,320
I think, you know, a lot of the apps that we see today, they're coming online.

41
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,480
And I love, you know, the entrepreneur, you're all, you know, kind of red is sauce.

42
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,680
We're seeing a lot of people, they're being able to create new stuff.

43
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:06,800
But I think also it's important that people understand that there needs to be more than that to be able to build massive companies.

44
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:17,440
And so that's the, that's the shift that we've been on where, you know, what we want to do is we want to make sure we can create a product that is on path to be a billion user product.

45
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:29,920
And so, you know, starts with a team, like figuring out the right kind of team, building a brand, building a product that has a lot of retention, and then figuring out how to actually use AI to improve the value we give to users.

46
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:30,400
Yeah.

47
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:35,600
A quote from your talk that I really loved was, Teams create product soul.

48
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,440
You can't vibe code a brand.

49
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:38,320
I love that.

50
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:39,760
Like, what's your thinking there?

51
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:40,080
Yeah.

52
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:50,880
Yeah, you know, I think maybe sometimes people think that apps are just like, you know, tools, but actually people want to connect with the apps that they use.

53
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,400
They want to, they want to feel something.

54
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:54,640
So, you know, the brand is super important.

55
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:59,200
I think some of the best, you know, apps out there like have, have this soul in them.

56
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:05,520
And I think that in order to have that soul, you really need kind of people that are really caring about this.

57
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:08,560
And, you know, it doesn't have to be like having, it doesn't have to be a lot of people.

58
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:13,200
You know, some very small teams create like really soulful products.

59
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,280
But I think that's a recipe for, for, for.

60
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:20,960
So the best SAP for a failure in my mind is just to create something that has a function, which AI does really, really well.

61
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:25,200
Like you can basically create, you know, note taking apps, you can create, like, calorie apps, you can write, like, whatever you want.

62
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:26,240
That's just a function.

63
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:30,560
But you need more than that to be able to be successful in my mind.

64
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:31,280
Yeah.

65
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,600
And one last AI take before we kind of move on, but back to, like, more Opal specific stuff.

66
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:46,000
What are your thoughts on, you know, people saying AI is going to eat all of consumer apps that, you know, and you've seen this.

67
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:50,000
I mean, competition for in the screen time space.

68
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:56,160
And then AI has unlocked the ability to spin up a screen time app faster than ever.

69
00:05:56,640 --> 00:06:01,440
And that's actually a popular category that I see a ton of indies launching into.

70
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:10,400
So how do you think about the way that's going to impact Opel's business and then how do you think that's going to impact the app economy more broadly?

71
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:13,040
Yeah, there's a lot of there's a lot of screen time apps.

72
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:24,640
You know, since we went on the podcast, actually someone came to the conference last week and walked up to me and said, you know, that she was super inspired by Opel and had created her own version of a screen time app.

73
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:28,400
And I think it's one of the kind of very popular categories these days.

74
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:36,400
The thing is that you, first of all, I think the way I see competition is I think that it's actually a net positive for us.

75
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:44,400
I think what it does is it helps more people realize that this problem is real, educate the market, get more people to use apps.

76
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:54,640
And there's, you know, there's five billion people in the world that have a smartphone and maybe today, probably, you know, give or take a few dozen million that have used a screen time app or are using a screen time app.

77
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:57,760
So there's still, you know, kind of massive opportunity to grow.

78
00:06:57,920 --> 00:06:59,840
I think the challenge, the problem is that when.

79
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:04,480
when a lot of these apps are actually kind of copying existing stuff.

80
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:17,280
So instead of trying to build out new categories and kind of reach out to newer kinds of people, they're limiting themselves by just copying what exists.

81
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:33,200
And so in my mind, the biggest challenge for screen time is if I walk down the street and I talk to ten people in any city, it could be in Texas and New York, wherever it is, and I ask people, a random set of people, like, do you spend too much time on your phone?

82
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,040
Everyone will say, you know, yes.

83
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:37,280
Yeah, or maybe like eight out of ten, right?

84
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:39,840
But then the next question is, there's an app, right?

85
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:41,760
Would you download this app to help you?

86
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:48,400
Today, you'd lose a lot of people because a lot of people say, what do you mean an app to what to do?

87
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:50,320
How does it help me manage my screen time?

88
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:51,680
Is it going to be blocking apps?

89
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:52,720
Like, that's crazy.

90
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:54,720
That's not really something that I want to do.

91
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:09,440
And so the problem that I think we need to address as a category is like, how do we actually create something that these people can use that's not, you know, what Opel already does, but that's like what, you know, other things.

92
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:23,360
And that's really what we're trying to build for Opel, which is like figuring out how we can make it so much easier to use a screen time app, no setup required, and that it's not just about restriction, that it's really about being able to kind of coach you in your day to day.

93
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:29,280
So that's kind of a long-winded answer, but I think that, you know, I applaud like competition.

94
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:44,800
I think it helps educate the market, but I think it's also, you know, the opportunity is much larger and it's going to require innovation and new kinds of apps and completely new kinds of UI that you're probably not going to get by just, you know, typing in.

95
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:48,400
in cloud like, make an Opal copy in five minutes, right?

96
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:48,960
Yeah.

97
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:53,200
And we've seen this play out for years even without AI coding.

98
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:55,680
It's not that hard.

99
00:08:56,160 --> 00:09:04,320
The engineering side is a lot of hours, but it's not some insurmountable thing and it never has been.

100
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:17,440
And so you look at some of the big categories like language learning, Duolingo, despite the recent setback in its stock price, is still a multibillion dollar company making over a billion dollars a year.

101
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:20,000
And then you look at that category and there are just.

102
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:22,240
so many apps and huge ones.

103
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:26,640
Babel is massive, right up there nipping on their heels.

104
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:42,240
And so I think, you know, for the broader industry and then for Opel specifically, you're still going to have category winners like Opel that are like the brand in the space, even if there are hundreds of apps that do the same thing.

105
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:53,920
And to your point earlier, it's like, yeah, you can copy Opal pretty, you know, or easier than ever to copy aspects of Opal, but you can't copy the soul.

106
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:56,480
You can't copy the taste that went into it.

107
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,320
And as people experiment with these apps, they'll probably be hungry for more and then go look for the best app to do it.

108
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:19,280
And I think, you know, Opal as kind of the leader in the space and as you've grown the team and improved the product, it just becomes this like momentum that isn't going to get stopped by two hundred more people vibe coding another screen time app.

109
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:19,760
Yeah.

110
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:22,720
I mean, I can bring you in my next investor pitch.

111
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:23,280
I love that.

112
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:26,800
No, I fully believe it.

113
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:36,160
And then one more thing is, you know, we talked about app what happened since the last podcast and when I think I mentioned this and we had a hard paywall, now we're truly freemium.

114
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:37,760
So we give away the product for free.

115
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:48,240
And that's also something that I think is very valuable for us in the long run because we're getting to a point where we passed a million DAUs, as you said, and we're growing really fast.

116
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:55,280
And it comes to a point where people have heard about the app and they hear it from their friends.

117
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,640
And we see a lot of interesting dynamics of students that are using it together.

118
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:10,320
We see interesting dynamics also in the family world where you have maybe a team teenager, a new teenager in the family that's using the app and actually recommending it to their parents, all the way around.

119
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:18,400
And so all of that makes it, you know, makes our position stronger and activates these network effects.

120
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:20,400
And it's really powered by freemium.

121
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:26,400
And I think it's, that's why I'm very confident in the future of consumer apps in general.

122
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:33,680
I think when you have a great product that has retention, that has a great brand, you're in an excellent position.

123
00:11:33,920 --> 00:12:05,200
And I think what you can do on top of that is you can use artificial intelligence to actually improve this product and by improving this product what I mean is not saying just moving the metrics it's really like how do you actually really improve the value that you give to users and if you're very confident the value that you give to users that whether it's like giving them time back which is kind of what we do or making them more productive whether it's you know helping learn language you can actually use AI to make that better and that really will make your product a lot stronger.

124
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:08,160
And so I think, you know, it's interesting.

125
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:22,240
I'm sure you see this all the time, maybe in the public markets at least, you know, the apps, software, SAS of course, and also some consumer software is getting really punished by the market to say, hey, they're not kind of AI native.

126
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:28,240
But I think that it's a bit of a I think it's a misunderstanding of what consumer apps really are.

127
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:31,520
It's not just function, it's also entertainment.

128
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:35,760
It's also something that people like to do and something that people love.

129
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:41,840
And so you can't just replace that with an agent or something else that does it automatically.

130
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:48,320
I wanted to dig a little deeper into the switch to freemium because that was a big deal for Opel.

131
00:12:48,480 --> 00:13:07,920
You shared a slide in your presentation showing that your download to paid dropped from twenty percent all the way down to nine percent, which sounds super scary to have your conversion more than cut in half, but why why did that ultimately end up being good for the company?

132
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:09,360
No, we did that.

133
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:11,920
So we gave it away, the product for free.

134
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:23,120
And you mentioned that, you know, our paid penetration, so that's how many people, you know, are paying subscribers within the monthly active users, you know, went from about twenty percent to nine percent.

135
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:27,360
And that's scary when you, when you see it this chart, but it was the right decision.

136
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:35,200
The reason it's the right decision is because when you give the product away for free, the people that are using the product become your marketing.

137
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:42,000
And so what we've seen as a consequence is also a shift where we have a lot more organic growth than we ever have.

138
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:45,040
It's massively increased.

139
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:52,240
And what we're able to do is we're able to kind of accelerate this organic growth as well with some features we can talk about.

140
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:59,600
Another thing that we've seen is as a consequence of a freedom is that we have a different kind of new kind of user, a new segment that.

141
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:06,560
I mean, we started with a segment of, I would say, professional pros that are interested in productivity.

142
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:14,640
You know, people like you and me, people probably listening to this podcast that, you know, care about their time and place a high ROI and like saving time.

143
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:21,440
So that was like the initial kind of segment, but, you know, making the app free really helped us unlock students as a second kind of segment.

144
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:27,360
So high school students and college students were now two thirds of our DAUs are students.

145
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:38,480
And what it does is not only it's great for our mission because we know and we really want, you know, students to be able to build these healthy habits, healthy digital habits in their day to day.

146
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:44,800
So that's great for the mission, but it's also great for organic growth because students talk about it.

147
00:14:45,200 --> 00:15:01,360
And what this also allowed us to do is now we're entering a third segment that's being unlocked where students basically, especially in high school, are telling their schools, hey, like, you know, instead of taking the phones away while we're in school, why don't you work with OPAL to be able to create a phone policy?

148
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:05,200
And so now we also have this third segment with schools that's opening.

149
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:19,520
So what I would say is I think that the freemium strategy is really helpful to be able to grow organically and also unlock new segments that aren't necessarily people that would actually pay upfront for products.

150
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:43,440
And so it depends on what you're looking for as an app founder, whether you're trying to build lifestyle business, but if you're trying to really create a product that has the potential to get used by hundreds of millions of people or billions of people, which is really what we think we must do because of the size of the problem we're facing, then I think freemium is a really good strategy to get there.

151
00:15:43,680 --> 00:15:43,760
Yeah.

152
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:56,080
So to sum up, we've seen a decrease in our conversion to paid, but we've also seen as a consequence of that an explosion of our DAUs and revenue with it.

153
00:15:56,640 --> 00:16:04,240
So it's a short term, scary drop, but what happens in the long term is that payback is it pays back tenfold.

154
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:05,120
That's amazing.

155
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:13,280
Another thing you did share in that presentation is a question you asked yourself of where to draw the line of freemium.

156
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:18,240
And I think this is a little counterintuitive, but I think really important to dig into.

157
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:22,080
You said, would a nonpayer recommend the app?

158
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:24,240
If not, give away more.

159
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:25,440
Why?

160
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:38,800
Yeah, I think if you want the freemium dynamic to really pay out, you need to make sure that the free users are recommending the app, otherwise that doesn't work.

161
00:16:39,280 --> 00:16:42,560
I think that's a good way to measure the quality of your freemium product.

162
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:47,360
You got to figure out whether free users are going to recommend the app.

163
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:54,080
And I think that's a problem with a lot of apps I see where they're just giving away a small amount.

164
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:58,000
Or maybe it feels like a trial and then it's kind of recapped.

165
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:03,920
If you do that, you probably not going to benefit from the organic growth potential of freemium.

166
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:05,120
Yeah, totally.

167
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:08,640
It's a tough balance of what to give away and what not.

168
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:19,520
Were there, were there any specific tough calls that you all made where it's like, this really could, should be behind the paywall, but like it really is such a good part of the experience that we don't want to take it away from the free users.

169
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:23,680
Like, what were some of the tough decisions and, and did you have to reverse any?

170
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:29,520
Like, did you make a bad call and then reverse it or how, how did things go on determining what went into pay?

171
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:34,640
Yeah, I mean, we've made a lot of, uh, made a lot of mistakes and, you know, some success.

172
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:40,320
I think the core is to measure, you know, experiment and measure two things.

173
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:50,880
So, you know, the LTV of installs, so how people are converting and paying, and also to measure the retention overall free plus paid, right?

174
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:56,320
And so you want to, you want to make sure that you can find the sweet spot where you're increasing both.

175
00:17:56,640 --> 00:17:57,280
It's really hard.

176
00:17:57,920 --> 00:18:01,440
We have this, um, these, uh, this feature called blocks.

177
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:04,720
So basically it's, you know, you're blocking apps from 90 to 100.

178
00:18:04,720 --> 00:18:08,320
from nine to ten on Monday to Friday, you're blocking social media, for example.

179
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:12,560
And also it could be, you know, you put together like an open limit.

180
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:16,480
So that's, you know, I can only open Instagram ten times during the day.

181
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:18,080
And so each of these are blocks.

182
00:18:18,240 --> 00:18:21,680
And so, you know, we've experimented with, you know, how many should we give away for free?

183
00:18:22,240 --> 00:18:24,480
You can have one or you can have two or three or four.

184
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:26,160
And I think today it's actually three.

185
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:31,840
We've tried many different approaches all the way from like having just one, which is pretty restrictive, and offering more.

186
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:38,080
And it turns out that three is enough for free users to get a great experience.

187
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:39,360
They can actually reuse that.

188
00:18:39,360 --> 00:18:47,600
Really use that app, try out a few different things and then also really power users that are convinced, you know, will pay because they want more.

189
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,640
So that's one where it kind of worked out pretty well.

190
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:51,840
Yeah, that's a great example.

191
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:54,400
And maybe a way to think about that.

192
00:18:54,560 --> 00:19:00,880
Yeah, it's like the people who are going to be the heaviest users, you know, what's the thing that's most important to them?

193
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:06,960
And then throwing it to where the free users still get a great experience is, yeah, is interesting.

194
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:16,400
I mean, and this AI is making this so tough with freemium because you, you almost can't give things away and it's hard to make a freemium app.

195
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:27,120
Like I've been vibe coding this app on the side and I'm, I'm working on this kind of AI coaching feature and golly, it can get super expensive.

196
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:33,120
And, and, you know, at first I started out like, oh, I'm going to use like Opus 4.6 because it's so much better.

197
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:35,120
And then I was like, oh, maybe I'll use Sonnet.

198
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:37,920
And then I was like, oh, maybe I'll use Gemini Flash.

199
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:54,320
And, um, and it's, it's tough these days to, to really draw those lines and create that great freemium experience, especially if you're using AI, but even if you're not using AI, like it's just such an important place to really be thoughtful and to do some experimentation.

200
00:19:54,560 --> 00:20:00,160
You know, like you said, you didn't just land on three by magic, like guessing it or whatever.

201
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:04,720
You tried one, you tried five and you figured it out.

202
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:09,120
And I think that's what people need to take away, like you just need to try it.

203
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:18,160
And for me, like vibe coding this app, like I need to see how users actually use it, because maybe I can get away with a better model and more coaching and things like that.

204
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:23,360
And maybe I can do a freemium tier, but I don't know until I actually get it out there and start testing it.

205
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:23,600
Yeah.

206
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:34,000
Yeah, I mean, I think it changes the equation because you're, you're of course paying for the service of using, if you're using a server side model, you're paying for that.

207
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:40,720
So you need to make sure that, you know, the value that you're creating is actually like higher than, of course, like the cost that you incur.

208
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:44,800
And I feel like a lot of people, you know, I think at the beginning, I'm this way too.

209
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:48,720
You're like, you know, the value is so cool of all these tools.

210
00:20:48,880 --> 00:20:53,120
You know, you're just going all in and building incredible stuff.

211
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:57,600
And then there's probably like a second phase, which is wait, like, does it make, you know, economic sense actually?

212
00:20:57,840 --> 00:20:59,200
Like, can I charge enough?

213
00:20:59,520 --> 00:20:59,680
for this?

214
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:00,800
Are people actually willing?

215
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:03,680
Is it good enough that people are willing to kind of pay for this on top?

216
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:12,560
So it's a different kind of reality, I guess, than when you were just making an app that's not, that doesn't have actually like this kind of marginal cost on top of it.

217
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:19,680
But yeah, coming back to, I think, AI, the interesting, it's massively, you know, valuable, obviously, and we see some incredible things.

218
00:21:20,080 --> 00:21:23,520
I'm like everyone fully using a lot of these tools.

