00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:50,720
Totally organically.

254
00:23:51,440 --> 00:24:00,640
I had like a fear that one day maybe we'lld work with colleges, but working with schools, particularly high school, was not something I thought about.

255
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:03,680
But what happened was it started last year.

256
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:17,520
We literally got an email from a high school in Los Angeles, Harvard Westlake, that essentially what happened is they had asked their students, basically the state is telling us to ban phones, what should we do?

257
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:24,160
And students got back to them and said, well, we all or many of us are using this app called Opal.

258
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:25,280
We're using it on our own.

259
00:24:25,920 --> 00:24:28,320
Nobody's telling us to do that, but maybe you should contact them.

260
00:24:28,720 --> 00:24:31,840
And so they had reached out to us to see if we could do something together.

261
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:35,200
And we found that, you know, it was interesting actually to do that.

262
00:24:35,680 --> 00:24:40,000
And so the way what we've done is we built Opal for schools.

263
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:46,240
What it is is it's a phone policy that schools can use to help their students focus and thrive.

264
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:50,320
And the way it works is the students download the Opal app.

265
00:24:50,800 --> 00:25:06,080
And then when they walk into campus, when they walk into school, the banned apps are blocked in their phone, but they can still access the apps that are authorized, which is often being able to text, you know, mom and dad., but also educational apps.

266
00:25:06,320 --> 00:25:16,080
And so, for example, in some circumstances, in some cases, school would say, Chat GPT can be used or this specific kind of physics apps, this calculator app.

267
00:25:16,400 --> 00:25:26,400
And the interesting part is that when they leave school, instead of just being headed back to their phone and being able to go and just binge on the social media, they still have the app.

268
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:35,760
And so what we see is that a lot of students are actually still using the app after school to set up their sleep schedule to be able to do focus timers and study.

269
00:25:35,920 --> 00:26:15,280
And so it's really kind of a not a belt toell solution but a bell to bed solution it's kind of a full cycle and so yeah we've tried it with you know one school and and now we're actually kind of scaling it across the states with many high schools around the US and so it's really become a very interesting new area for us not only because again super aligned we're on mission we really really like that I think it's a whole chapter we could open but it's a much smarter way to actually do phone policy rather than taking the phones away we're actually kind of teaching something to kids to get a tool that they can use after school.

270
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:22,400
And from a business perspective, it makes sense because it really helps position Opel in the family.

271
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:29,440
People talk about it on a local community level as well because every student uses it, so it helps a lot as well.

272
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:47,040
But yes, it's a new kind of distribution for us that is super interesting and extremely mission aligned because, you know, we need to make sure that education is still and continues to be an important vector for progress and we can be a part of that.

273
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:57,040
Another aspect to it too is that those students will eventually graduate and become professionals and want the tools and no longer have it provided by the school.

274
00:26:57,280 --> 00:27:06,400
So it's also kind of like growing up that next generation of pro users who are professionals who can afford the more expensive tier and stuff.

275
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:07,680
I was very impressed.

276
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:29,840
I remember my college days when our college library, we used to have PCs and then they kind of did renovation and all of a sudden they had 100% Mac everywhere, you know, this kind of newest kind of beautiful, beautiful Mac that had been, I guess, like practically given away by Apple to kind of, you know, be, be present in college.

277
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:38,800
And yeah, I think, I think obviously, you know, from a business perspective, it makes sense to make sure that the next generation knows about the products and can use them and can and loves them.

